  ('ai-engineering', 5, 'How do Vector Databases index high-dimensional embeddings using HNSW (Hierarchical Navigable Small World)?', 'Advanced', 'HNSW constructs a multi-layer graph hierarchy where upper layers have longer skip links (like a skip-list) and bottom layers contain dense local nearest-neighbor graphs. Queries start at top layer, perform greedy search to local minimum, drop down to next layer, achieving approximate $O(\log N)$ search latency for million-scale vector spaces.', '**Difficulty**: Advanced

**Strategy**:
HNSW constructs a multi-layer graph hierarchy where upper layers have longer skip links (like a skip-list) and bottom layers contain dense local nearest-neighbor graphs. Queries start at top layer, perform greedy search to local minimum, drop down to next layer, achieving approximate $O(\log N)$ search latency for million-scale vector spaces.

**Code Example**:
```text
HNSW Layer Hierarchy:
Layer 2: [Node A] --------------------> [Node Z] (Express Highway)
Layer 1: [Node A] ---------> [Node M] ---------> [Node Z]
Layer 0: [Node A]->[Node B]->[Node C]... (Dense All Nodes, Exact Neighbors)
```

