}', CURRENT_TIMESTAMP),
  ('css', 9, 'How do you use Container Queries to build component-based responsive styles?', 'Intermediate', 'Container queries are a paradigm shift from media queries because they enable truly reusable components that respond to their own container size, not the viewport. This is critical for design systems where the same card component may appear in a sidebar or a full-width page. You must declare `container-type` on the parent before `@container` rules will work. This feature shows interviewers you keep up with modern CSS evolution.', '**Difficulty**: Intermediate

**Strategy**:
Container queries are a paradigm shift from media queries because they enable truly reusable components that respond to their own container size, not the viewport. This is critical for design systems where the same card component may appear in a sidebar or a full-width page. You must declare `container-type` on the parent before `@container` rules will work. This feature shows interviewers you keep up with modern CSS evolution.

**Code Example**:
```css
.card-container {
  container-type: inline-size;
}

.card {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
}

/* If container is wider than 400px */
@container (min-width: 400px) {
  .card {
    flex-direction: row;
  }
}
```

---

