  ('css', 12, 'How do you ensure an element is visually hidden but accessible to screen readers?', 'Intermediate', 'The `.sr-only` (screen-reader-only) pattern is a staple of accessible web development and appears in every major CSS framework. Using `display: none` or `visibility: hidden` completely removes the element from the accessibility tree, making it invisible to assistive technology. The clip-based approach keeps the element in the DOM and accessible while rendering it invisible on screen. This class is essential for providing context to screen readers on icon buttons and skip-navigation links.', '**Difficulty**: Intermediate

**Strategy**:
The `.sr-only` (screen-reader-only) pattern is a staple of accessible web development and appears in every major CSS framework. Using `display: none` or `visibility: hidden` completely removes the element from the accessibility tree, making it invisible to assistive technology. The clip-based approach keeps the element in the DOM and accessible while rendering it invisible on screen. This class is essential for providing context to screen readers on icon buttons and skip-navigation links.

**Code Example**:
```css
.sr-only {
  position: absolute;
  width: 1px;
  height: 1px;
  padding: 0;
  margin: -1px;
  overflow: hidden;
  clip: rect(0, 0, 0, 0);
  white-space: nowrap;
  border: 0;
}
```

---

<a id="q13"></a>', '.sr-only {
  position: absolute;
  width: 1px;
  height: 1px;
  padding: 0;
  margin: -1px;
  overflow: hidden;
  clip: rect(0, 0, 0, 0);
  white-space: nowrap;
  border: 0;
}', CURRENT_TIMESTAMP),
  ('css', 13, 'How do you implement a CSS Triangle?', 'Intermediate', 'CSS triangles are a classic interview trick question that tests your understanding of the CSS box model and border rendering. When an element has zero width and height, borders from opposing sides meet at diagonal edges, creating triangular shapes. By making three borders transparent and one colored, you control which triangle is visible. While largely replaced by `clip-path` and SVG in production, this technique still appears in legacy codebases and demonstrates deep CSS knowledge.', '**Difficulty**: Intermediate

**Strategy**:
CSS triangles are a classic interview trick question that tests your understanding of the CSS box model and border rendering. When an element has zero width and height, borders from opposing sides meet at diagonal edges, creating triangular shapes. By making three borders transparent and one colored, you control which triangle is visible. While largely replaced by `clip-path` and SVG in production, this technique still appears in legacy codebases and demonstrates deep CSS knowledge.

**Code Example**:
```css
.triangle-up {
  width: 0;
  height: 0;
  border-left: 25px solid transparent;
  border-right: 25px solid transparent;
  border-bottom: 50px solid #555;
}

.triangle-right {
  width: 0;
  height: 0;
  border-top: 25px solid transparent;
  border-bottom: 25px solid transparent;
  border-left: 50px solid #555;
}
```

---

<a id="q14"></a>', '.triangle-up {
  width: 0;
