  align-items: center;
}', CURRENT_TIMESTAMP),
  ('css', 20, 'How do you use `minmax()` in CSS Grid?', 'Intermediate', '`minmax()` is the key to building truly responsive grids without media queries. Combined with `auto-fit` or `auto-fill`, it creates a grid that automatically wraps items into as many columns as will fit. `minmax(200px, 1fr)` means each column is at least 200px wide but shares any extra space equally. The difference between `auto-fit` (collapses empty tracks) and `auto-fill` (preserves empty tracks) is a common follow-up question in interviews.', '**Difficulty**: Intermediate

**Strategy**:
`minmax()` is the key to building truly responsive grids without media queries. Combined with `auto-fit` or `auto-fill`, it creates a grid that automatically wraps items into as many columns as will fit. `minmax(200px, 1fr)` means each column is at least 200px wide but shares any extra space equally. The difference between `auto-fit` (collapses empty tracks) and `auto-fill` (preserves empty tracks) is a common follow-up question in interviews.

**Code Example**:
```css
.grid {
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(200px, 1fr));
}
```

---

<a id="q21"></a>', '.grid {
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(200px, 1fr));
}', CURRENT_TIMESTAMP),
  ('css', 21, 'How do you create overlapping elements with CSS Grid?', 'Intermediate', 'One of CSS Grid''s unique strengths is that multiple items can occupy the same grid cell, creating overlapping layouts without `position: absolute`. This is useful for hero sections, image overlays, and decorative elements. Later DOM elements naturally stack on top of earlier ones, and `z-index` controls the order within the same stacking context. This approach is more maintainable than absolute positioning because items remain in the document flow.', '**Difficulty**: Intermediate

**Strategy**:
One of CSS Grid''s unique strengths is that multiple items can occupy the same grid cell, creating overlapping layouts without `position: absolute`. This is useful for hero sections, image overlays, and decorative elements. Later DOM elements naturally stack on top of earlier ones, and `z-index` controls the order within the same stacking context. This approach is more maintainable than absolute positioning because items remain in the document flow.

**Code Example**:
```css
.item1 { grid-column: 1 / 3; grid-row: 1; }
.item2 { grid-column: 2 / 4; grid-row: 1; opacity: 0.8; }
```

