.item2 { grid-column: 2 / 4; grid-row: 1; opacity: 0.8; }', CURRENT_TIMESTAMP),
  ('css', 22, 'How do you use `z-index` and stacking contexts effectively?', 'Intermediate', 'Stacking contexts are one of the most misunderstood CSS concepts and a frequent source of bugs in complex UIs. A stacking context is created not just by `z-index` but also by `opacity` less than 1, `transform`, `filter`, `will-change`, and several other properties. Once a stacking context is created, `z-index` values of children cannot escape it to overlap elements in a sibling context. Understanding this prevents the "z-index arms race" of ever-increasing values.', '**Difficulty**: Intermediate

**Strategy**:
Stacking contexts are one of the most misunderstood CSS concepts and a frequent source of bugs in complex UIs. A stacking context is created not just by `z-index` but also by `opacity` less than 1, `transform`, `filter`, `will-change`, and several other properties. Once a stacking context is created, `z-index` values of children cannot escape it to overlap elements in a sibling context. Understanding this prevents the "z-index arms race" of ever-increasing values.

**Code Example**:
```css
.top {
  position: relative;
  z-index: 10;
}
```

---

<a id="q23"></a>', '.top {
