  ('fintech', 5, 'What is Simple Binary Encoding (SBE) and how does it outperform JSON and Protobuf in financial market data feeds?', 'Advanced', 'SBE encodes fields at fixed binary byte offsets matching native hardware word alignments. Unlike Protobuf (which requires variable-length varint parsing) or JSON (which requires text parsing), SBE requires zero decoding logic: data fields are accessed by direct pointer casting (`*(uint64_t*)(buf + offset)`), achieving zero-allocation and single-cycle CPU reads.', '**Difficulty**: Advanced

**Strategy**:
SBE encodes fields at fixed binary byte offsets matching native hardware word alignments. Unlike Protobuf (which requires variable-length varint parsing) or JSON (which requires text parsing), SBE requires zero decoding logic: data fields are accessed by direct pointer casting (`*(uint64_t*)(buf + offset)`), achieving zero-allocation and single-cycle CPU reads.

**Code Example**:
```cpp
// Direct Zero-Copy SBE Field Access via Memory Pointer
#pragma pack(push, 1)
struct MarketUpdateSBE {
    uint16_t msg_type;
    uint64_t timestamp_ns;
    uint32_t symbol_id;
    int64_t  price_mantissa;
    int8_t   price_exponent;
    uint32_t quantity;
};
#pragma pack(pop)
```

---

