  ('html', 7, 'How does the `<canvas>` API differ from `<svg>`?', 'Intermediate', '- **`<canvas>`**: Raster/pixel-based, immediate mode rendering (draws pixels, does not retain DOM nodes), high performance for 100,000+ particles and gaming, requires manual redraw on zoom.
- **`<svg>`**: Vector-based, retained mode rendering (each shape is an XML DOM element with event listeners and CSS styling), resolution-independent, ideal for interactive charts and UI icons.', '**Difficulty**: Intermediate

**Strategy**:
- **`<canvas>`**: Raster/pixel-based, immediate mode rendering (draws pixels, does not retain DOM nodes), high performance for 100,000+ particles and gaming, requires manual redraw on zoom.
- **`<svg>`**: Vector-based, retained mode rendering (each shape is an XML DOM element with event listeners and CSS styling), resolution-independent, ideal for interactive charts and UI icons.

**Code Example**:
```html
<!-- Canvas (Raster) -->
<canvas id="cv" width="200" height="100"></canvas>
<script>
const ctx = document.getElementById(''cv'').getContext(''2d'');
ctx.fillStyle = ''#22c55e'';
ctx.fillRect(10, 10, 80, 80);
</script>

<!-- SVG (Vector) -->
<svg width="200" height="100">
  <rect x="10" y="10" width="80" height="80" fill="#3b82f6" />
</svg>
```

---

