  ('kubernetes', 86, 'How do you limit jobs history?', 'Intermediate', 'Completed Jobs and CronJobs accumulate in the cluster over time, consuming etcd storage and cluttering `kubectl get jobs` output if not cleaned up automatically. Use `ttlSecondsAfterFinished` on Jobs to have Kubernetes garbage-collect them after completion, and configure `successfulJobsHistoryLimit` / `failedJobsHistoryLimit` on CronJobs to cap retained records. A common pitfall is not setting these limits on CronJobs that run every minute -- without cleanup, thousands of completed Job objects can slow down the API server and exhaust etcd space.', '**Difficulty**: Intermediate

**Strategy**:
Completed Jobs and CronJobs accumulate in the cluster over time, consuming etcd storage and cluttering `kubectl get jobs` output if not cleaned up automatically. Use `ttlSecondsAfterFinished` on Jobs to have Kubernetes garbage-collect them after completion, and configure `successfulJobsHistoryLimit` / `failedJobsHistoryLimit` on CronJobs to cap retained records. A common pitfall is not setting these limits on CronJobs that run every minute -- without cleanup, thousands of completed Job objects can slow down the API server and exhaust etcd space.

**Code Example**:
```yaml
ttlSecondsAfterFinished: 100
```

