<a id="q46"></a>', 'import { Button } from ''design-system''', CURRENT_TIMESTAMP),
  ('microfrontend', 46, 'What is Code Splitting?', 'Beginner', 'Code splitting breaks a large JavaScript bundle into smaller chunks that are loaded on demand rather than upfront. This is fundamental to microfrontend performance because it ensures users only download the code they need for the current view. Route-based splitting and dynamic `import()` are the most common approaches; the main pitfall is over-splitting, which creates too many small HTTP requests and can actually hurt performance on slow networks.', '**Difficulty**: Beginner

**Strategy**:
Code splitting breaks a large JavaScript bundle into smaller chunks that are loaded on demand rather than upfront. This is fundamental to microfrontend performance because it ensures users only download the code they need for the current view. Route-based splitting and dynamic `import()` are the most common approaches; the main pitfall is over-splitting, which creates too many small HTTP requests and can actually hurt performance on slow networks.

**Code Example**:
```javascript
import()
```

---

<a id="q47"></a>', 'import()', CURRENT_TIMESTAMP),
  ('microfrontend', 47, 'How do you handle fonts and assets?', 'Intermediate', 'Static assets like fonts, images, and icons should be centralized on a CDN and referenced by absolute URLs so every microfrontend loads the same version without duplication. The shell app is typically responsible for loading fonts to avoid flash-of-unstyled-text and redundant requests. A common mistake is bundling fonts inside each MFE, which bloats bundles and causes the browser to download the same font file multiple times.', '**Difficulty**: Intermediate

**Strategy**:
Static assets like fonts, images, and icons should be centralized on a CDN and referenced by absolute URLs so every microfrontend loads the same version without duplication. The shell app is typically responsible for loading fonts to avoid flash-of-unstyled-text and redundant requests. A common mistake is bundling fonts inside each MFE, which bloats bundles and causes the browser to download the same font file multiple times.

**Code Example**:
```javascript
url(''https://cdn.../font.woff'')
```

---

<a id="q48"></a>', 'url(''https://cdn.../font.woff'')', CURRENT_TIMESTAMP),
  ('microfrontend', 48, 'What is Federated Modules Loader?', 'Advanced', 'The Federated Modules Loader is Webpack''s internal runtime that resolves, loads, and shares modules across separately built applications at runtime. It handles the negotiation of shared dependencies, ensuring only one copy of a singleton library is loaded. Understanding this loader is important for debugging "shared module not available" errors, which typically occur when the loading order or version negotiation fails between the host and a remote.', '**Difficulty**: Advanced

**Strategy**:
The Federated Modules Loader is Webpack''s internal runtime that resolves, loads, and shares modules across separately built applications at runtime. It handles the negotiation of shared dependencies, ensuring only one copy of a singleton library is loaded. Understanding this loader is important for debugging "shared module not available" errors, which typically occur when the loading order or version negotiation fails between the host and a remote.

**Code Example**:
```javascript
// webpack/container/reference
```

---

<a id="q49"></a>', '// webpack/container/reference', CURRENT_TIMESTAMP),
  ('microfrontend', 49, 'What is Atomic Design?', 'Beginner', 'Atomic Design is a methodology for creating design systems. It breaks interfaces down into Atoms (buttons, inputs), Molecules (search form), Organisms (header), Templates, and Pages. This structure is highly effective for sharing UI components across Microfrontends.', '**Difficulty**: Beginner

**Strategy**:
Atomic Design is a methodology for creating design systems. It breaks interfaces down into Atoms (buttons, inputs), Molecules (search form), Organisms (header), Templates, and Pages. This structure is highly effective for sharing UI components across Microfrontends.

**Code Example**:
```javascript
// Atoms, Molecules, Organisms
```

