  ('rust', 1, 'Explain Rust''s Ownership, Borrowing, and Lifetimes system and how it guarantees Memory Safety without a GC?', 'Advanced', 'Rust enforces safety at compile time through 3 invariant rules:
1. Each value in Rust has an owner.
2. There can only be one owner at a time.
3. When the owner goes out of scope, the value is dropped.
Borrowing rules: You may have either one mutable reference (`&mut T`) OR any number of immutable references (`&T`), preventing data races. Lifetimes (`''a`) represent compile-time regions of code where references remain valid, preventing dangling pointers.', '**Difficulty**: Advanced

**Strategy**:
Rust enforces safety at compile time through 3 invariant rules:
1. Each value in Rust has an owner.
2. There can only be one owner at a time.
3. When the owner goes out of scope, the value is dropped.
Borrowing rules: You may have either one mutable reference (`&mut T`) OR any number of immutable references (`&T`), preventing data races. Lifetimes (`''a`) represent compile-time regions of code where references remain valid, preventing dangling pointers.

**Code Example**:
```rust
// Lifetime annotation ensuring reference does not outlive owner
fn longest<''a>(x: &''a str, y: &''a str) -> &''a str {
    if x.len() > y.len() { x } else { y }
}
```

