}', CURRENT_TIMESTAMP),
  ('rust', 3, 'What is `Pin<P>` and `Unpin` in Rust and why are they necessary for Asynchronous Futures?', 'Advanced', 'Async/await functions compile into self-referential generator state machines where a struct contains pointers to its own internal fields. If the struct moved in memory, self-referential pointers would become dangling. `Pin` guarantees that the pointed-to value will never be moved in memory until dropped, making self-referential async Futures safe.', '**Difficulty**: Advanced

**Strategy**:
Async/await functions compile into self-referential generator state machines where a struct contains pointers to its own internal fields. If the struct moved in memory, self-referential pointers would become dangling. `Pin` guarantees that the pointed-to value will never be moved in memory until dropped, making self-referential async Futures safe.

**Code Example**:
```rust
use std::pin::Pin;
use std::future::Future;

// Pinned Future trait object
type BoxFuture<''a, T> = Pin<Box<dyn Future<Output = T> + Send + ''a>>;
```

---

