  ('security', 24, 'How should session expiration work (idle vs absolute, sliding windows)?', 'Intermediate', 'Idle timeout logs out after inactivity (banking: 5–15min; normal apps: hours); absolute cap forces re-auth regardless of activity (8–24h). Sliding expiration extends the idle deadline per request — never past the absolute limit. High-risk actions should re-auth regardless. Server-side state makes revocation instant; if stateless JWTs, keep TTLs minutes-short with refresh rotation.', '**Difficulty**: Intermediate

**Strategy**:
Idle timeout logs out after inactivity (banking: 5–15min; normal apps: hours); absolute cap forces re-auth regardless of activity (8–24h). Sliding expiration extends the idle deadline per request — never past the absolute limit. High-risk actions should re-auth regardless. Server-side state makes revocation instant; if stateless JWTs, keep TTLs minutes-short with refresh rotation.

**Code Example**:
```javascript
const IDLE_MS = 30 * 60_000, ABSOLUTE_MS = 12 * 60 * 60_000;
function sessionValid(sess) {
  const now = Date.now();
  if (now - sess.issuedAt > ABSOLUTE_MS) return false;      // hard cap
  sess.lastSeen = now;                                       // sliding idle window
  return true;
}
```

---

<a id="q25"></a>', 'const IDLE_MS = 30 * 60_000, ABSOLUTE_MS = 12 * 60 * 60_000;
