  ('security', 81, 'HTTP request smuggling (CL.TE/TE.CL) — mechanism and defenses?', 'Expert', 'Front-end and back-end disagree on message boundaries when a request carries both Content-Length and Transfer-Encoding — attacker-crafted desync lets poisoned "prefixes" hijack the next user''s request (auth captured, cache poisoned). Defenses: HTTP/2 end-to-end where possible, normalize/reject requests containing both headers, identical header parsing across tiers (WAF+server config audit), and reject obs-fold/whitespace anomalies.', '**Difficulty**: Expert

**Strategy**:
Front-end and back-end disagree on message boundaries when a request carries both Content-Length and Transfer-Encoding — attacker-crafted desync lets poisoned "prefixes" hijack the next user''s request (auth captured, cache poisoned). Defenses: HTTP/2 end-to-end where possible, normalize/reject requests containing both headers, identical header parsing across tiers (WAF+server config audit), and reject obs-fold/whitespace anomalies.

**Code Example**:
```text
POST / HTTP/1.1
Content-Length: 6
Transfer-Encoding: chunked

0

X   # front-end (CL) forwards 6 bytes; back-end (TE) reads chunked -> "X" prefixes next request
```

