  ('typescript', 92, 'How do you keep `Promise.all`-style combinators precisely typed with heterogeneous tuples?', 'Intermediate', '`Promise.all` accepts iterables of promises **or** plain values and returns the positional tuple of unwrapped results — driven by mapped-tuple inference. Writing your own combinator requires mapping over the tuple (`{ [K in keyof T]: Awaited<T[K]> }`) — a classic exercise.', '**Difficulty**: Intermediate

**Strategy**:
`Promise.all` accepts iterables of promises **or** plain values and returns the positional tuple of unwrapped results — driven by mapped-tuple inference. Writing your own combinator requires mapping over the tuple (`{ [K in keyof T]: Awaited<T[K]> }`) — a classic exercise.

**Code Example**:
```typescript
const [user, settings, flag] = await Promise.all([
  fetchUser(),                    // Promise<User>
  fetchSettings(),                // Promise<Settings>
  true,                           // boolean passthrough
]); // tuple types preserved positionally

type All<T extends readonly unknown[]> = { [K in keyof T]: Awaited<T[K]> };
```

